<b>ט.</b> It is worthy of all, etc. - and the Kabbalists greatly extended the virtue of midnight rising because it is great. And the order of leadership according to the writings of the late Ari has already been printed in the siddurim. And midnight time is always through the night literally everywhere, and even in the long or short nights. And it's twelve hours past half the day. And the time to lament the destruction is more correct to be a little before midnight, and from midnight onwards he will study the Torah, and at the end of the night he will ask for his needs. And some say then "save for the morning". And if he is close to the hot hawk, he will say the psalms alone and skip the lamentations, and then say "Watching for the morning." And after the correction, a secondary lesson comes before everything. And if he gained the wisdom of the truth, that time is very capable. The latter accepted, from "Shabbi" to "Komi", we were from Shavuot when they say "Lie down" to Tisha B'Av when they say "Komi", there is no need to get up at night. And some say, from 10 Bab to 10 Bayer Yakum. And those who practice according to Kabbalah believe that you should always get up. A group of people who joined together to say tikkun chatzot in public, there is no monam, and rather, in the majority with the exclusion of a king. And it is better that they say the correction in the synagogue than that they say it in the room next to it, the Kaddish Tapi synagogue, and also that they be among the first ten. And one should especially be very, very careful about making small talk in their youth at night. And he who cannot wake up before the morning light, because he is weak in nature, or he knows in himself that if he gets up in detention he will sleep during the prayer, it is better to sleep as much as necessary. And in any case, he will be very careful to get up an hour, or at least half an hour, before the reading of the Shamash for the synagogue, so that he can prepare himself to pray in public and in public.<br>
<br>
<b>י.</b> May there be a strait - but the Torah and the prayer will be with joy.<br>
<br>
<b>יא.</b> The destruction of the Temple - the Lord wrote that at every meal he should say "On the rivers of Babylon"; And on Shabbat, as well as on days when supplication is not said, he will say "Song of virtues on the return of the Lord" and so on. And the main thing is that he knows what camer, and after the intention of the heart are the things.
